Yes—I think you’re describing making the exceptional ordinary in your own life.

It doesn’t have to be common to be legitimate. The unusual thing becomes “normal” when it fits your values, you can live with its consequences, and you stop treating other people’s unfamiliarity as evidence that you’re doing something wrong.

There’s a useful distinction, though: “normal for me” can mean a grounded personal norm, or it can become a permission slip that shields an impulse from scrutiny. A good check is whether you can explain the choice plainly, respect other people’s boundaries, and still hear honest feedback without feeling erased by it.

Maybe the phrase is: “Uncommon doesn’t mean wrong. I can make it ordinary in my life, responsibly.”